#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491918 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5826e975-df63-51e0-a67a-1164fd2d1f8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491968 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d59ee512-a410-5f72-bb29-840819934fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491988 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=1$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=1,$ which of the following must be true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ae5ac07-ecc2-5615-aa8d-7abad2a2df30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491988 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492011 #edb/ref 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,0).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c1df99e-2ead-55b4-87fb-2ae2dcf226a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492011 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492026 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{9}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx\\,dy.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7406d317-ea31-5b68-821e-2624f64676a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492026 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{9}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{9}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[\\,x\\,]_{{y}^{2}}^{9}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}(9-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{9}{y}-y)dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{9}{y}-y)dy & ={[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(9\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2})-(9\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =9-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(19-{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492036 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492036 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492036 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492036 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8de6a121-b818-5379-9a6e-e58e864f7095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492036 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492075 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492075 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01ad38cc-bb95-5695-afba-148911d71064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492075 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186275137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492078 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "185add21-7fca-5043-b1bb-77f875cdde19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492083 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "229570e5-48d3-5b00-84f2-f501e8e1af86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186491988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492098 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84a70949-91ec-573e-84c4-a48e1622c4df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492098 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492102 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cbfd95c-fb2c-5711-9241-0780511db5f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186256682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492110 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492110 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4f5260c-b21a-5537-b50f-a1f34f0c3fa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492110 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492112 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492112 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe828ca9-046e-5b1a-9cbd-cc293160e776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175